From f153e2246180bc16349b01e1396ad1c6ee990e78 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Satish Babariya <themadengineer99@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 19:23:50 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] Add default-deny egress policy to the virtual network

The gateway terminates every flow from the guest, which makes it the one
place egress can be enforced that the guest cannot reach. This adds an
optional policy engine at that point.

pkg/policy holds the matcher and a resolution ledger. Two gates use it:

  - DNS refuses to resolve a name outside policy, so the guest never learns
    its address, and records every address it does hand out.
  - The TCP and UDP forwarders refuse to dial an address unless our own
    resolver vouched for it under an allowed name, or the address itself is
    allow-listed. An address we never answered is refused, so hardcoding an
    IP to skip DNS fails closed instead of bypassing the check.

Deny is evaluated before allow everywhere, including across every name
sharing an address, so a denied name cannot be laundered through a second
name on the same CDN IP.

Policy is nil by default and every existing call path keeps its behaviour;
types.Configuration.Policy opts in, so the existing -config flag carries it.
A malformed rule fails startup rather than being ignored.

Matching semantics are pinned by testdata/host-patterns.json, shared
verbatim with the Swift implementation that renders airlock policy check.
